### Is this a regression?

- [ ] Yes, this behavior used to work in the previous version

### The previous version in which this bug was not present was

_No response_

### Description

`AccordionTrigger.ngOnDestroy()` reads the required `panel()` input even if the trigger is destroyed before its first change detection and before `ngOnInit` has run. Destroying a component synchronously after `TestBed.createComponent()` therefore throws NG0950, despite `[panel]` being bound correctly in the template.

This is an early-teardown lifecycle issue, for example in a test that creates a component without running change detection before teardown. I reproduced it with the repository Chromium runner. I have not verified how often it occurs in production applications. An initialized accordion is not affected by this reproduction.

### Reproduction

The minimal failing operation, using the fixture defined below, is:

```ts
const fixture = TestBed.createComponent(StaticAccordion);
fixture.destroy(); // NG0950: required input "panel" has not been set yet.
```

This is a synchronous creation/teardown lifecycle reproduction rather than a visible interaction. I am including complete TestBed fixtures and an exact repository test command instead of an interactive StackBlitz.

#### Setup and steps

1. Check out angular/components at `e59fce84b`. This includes the accordion listener changes from #34053.
2. Install Node.js from `.nvmrc` and pnpm from `package.json#packageManager`, then run `pnpm install --frozen-lockfile`.
3. Append the following fixture and tests to `src/aria/accordion/accordion.spec.ts` as a separate top-level `describe`, outside the existing `describe('AccordionGroup')`. Its `afterEach` expects its own initialized fixture. The imports listed below are already present in that file; reuse them rather than duplicating them.
4. Run `pnpm test src/aria/accordion --no-watch`.

```ts
// Already imported in accordion.spec.ts:
// import {Component, ChangeDetectionStrategy} from "@angular/core";
// import {TestBed} from "@angular/core/testing";
// import {AccordionGroup} from "./accordion-group";
// import {AccordionTrigger} from "./accordion-trigger";
// import {AccordionPanel} from "./accordion-panel";
// import {AccordionContent} from "./accordion-content";

@Component({
  template: `
    <div ngAccordionGroup>
      <button ngAccordionTrigger [panel]="panel">Trigger</button>
      <div ngAccordionPanel #panel="ngAccordionPanel">
        <ng-template ngAccordionContent>Content</ng-template>
      </div>
    </div>
  `,
  imports: [AccordionGroup, AccordionTrigger, AccordionPanel, AccordionContent],
  changeDetection: ChangeDetectionStrategy.Eager,
})
class StaticAccordion {}

describe("Accordion destruction", () => {
  it("destroys before first change detection", () => {
    const fixture = TestBed.createComponent(StaticAccordion);
    expect(() => fixture.destroy()).not.toThrow();
  });

  it("destroys after initialization (control)", async () => {
    const fixture = TestBed.createComponent(StaticAccordion);
    await fixture.whenStable();
    expect(() => fixture.destroy()).not.toThrow();
  });
});
```

Do not call `fixture.detectChanges()` or await `fixture.whenStable()` before destroying in the first test: that applies the input binding and bypasses the reproduction. In the control test, awaiting stability allows initialization and makes destruction succeed.

Keep the trigger and panel static in the template. Putting them inside `@if`/`@for` changes when they are created. The template does bind `[panel]` correctly; the issue is reading that binding before the first change detection has applied it.

### Expected Behavior

Destroying an accordion before initialization should complete without throwing. Since `ngOnInit` has not associated the trigger with a panel or registered it in the group, cleanup should not read its still-unset required input.

After initialization, destruction should continue clearing the panel association and unregistering the trigger.

### Actual Behavior

The early-destruction test fails with:

```text
Expected function not to throw, but it threw Error:
NG0950: Input "panel" is required but no value is available yet.
```

With the two tests in the reproduction, the Chromium accordion suite reports **44 passed, 1 failed**. Only the early-destruction test fails; the initialized control passes.

#### Cause and related PRs

[AccordionTrigger at the tested revision](https://github.com/angular/components/blob/e59fce84b/src/aria/accordion/accordion-trigger.ts) associates the panel in `ngOnInit` with `this.panel()._pattern = this._pattern`, but unconditionally reads `this.panel()` again in `ngOnDestroy`. The early-teardown path has not applied the required input binding yet.

The same lifecycle problem was fixed for combobox in #33974 (issue #33972). Tree has a separate report #34058 and proposed fix #34059; neither includes accordion.

The accordion-only implementation and regression tests are in #34061. It retains the panel associated during initialization and skips clearing it when no association was made; the initialized cleanup is also tested.

### Environment

- Components source: angular/components main at `e59fce84b` (2026-11-04)
- Angular framework packages used by the repository: 22.3.0-next.3
- Browser: Chromium provided by the repository Bazel browser test setup
- Operating system: macOS, Darwin 25.5.0, arm64
- Node.js: 22.23.4 (`.nvmrc`)
- pnpm: 12.16.1 (`packageManager`)
- Command: `pnpm test src/aria/accordion --no-watch`
- Regression status: unknown; older releases have not been tested.

### Is this a regression?

- [ ] Yes, this behavior used to work in the previous version

### The previous version in which this bug was not present was

_No response_

### Description

`TreeItem` and `TreeItemGroup` throw NG0950 when a component containing them is destroyed synchronously after `TestBed.createComponent()`, before its first change detection.

The required inputs are bound in the template. However, `ngOnInit` has not run and the input bindings have not been applied, while `ngOnDestroy` still reads them when attempting to unregister.

This affects early teardown, for example a unit test that creates a component and destroys it without running change detection. I reproduced this in the repository Chromium test runner. I have not verified how frequently it occurs in production applications; ordinary initialized trees are not affected by this reproduction.

### Reproduction

The minimal failing operation, using either fixture defined below, is:

```ts
const fixture = TestBed.createComponent(StaticFlatTree);
fixture.destroy(); // NG0950: required input "parent" has not been set yet.
```

With `StaticNestedTree`, the same operation reports the required `ownedBy` input.

This is a synchronous creation/teardown lifecycle reproduction rather than a visible interaction, so I am including complete TestBed fixtures and the exact repository test command instead of an interactive StackBlitz.

#### Repository setup

1. Check out angular/components at `e59fce84b`.
2. Install the Node.js version in `.nvmrc` and pnpm from `package.json#packageManager`, then run `pnpm install --frozen-lockfile`.
3. In `src/aria/tree/tree.spec.ts`, add the following fixtures and tests as a separate top-level `describe`, outside the existing `describe('Tree')` (its `afterEach` expects its own initialized fixture). The imports listed below are already present; reuse them instead of adding duplicate imports.
4. Run `pnpm test src/aria/tree --no-watch`.

```ts
// Already imported in tree.spec.ts:
// import {Component, ChangeDetectionStrategy} from "@angular/core";
// import {TestBed} from "@angular/core/testing";
// import {Tree} from "./tree";
// import {TreeItem} from "./tree-item";
// import {TreeItemGroup} from "./tree-item-group";

@Component({
  template: `
    <ul ngTree #tree="ngTree">
      <li ngTreeItem [parent]="tree" value="item">Item</li>
    </ul>
  `,
  imports: [Tree, TreeItem],
  changeDetection: ChangeDetectionStrategy.Eager,
})
class StaticFlatTree {}

@Component({
  template: `
    <ul ngTree #tree="ngTree">
      <li ngTreeItem [parent]="tree" value="parent" #parentItem="ngTreeItem">
        Parent
        <ul role="group">
          <ng-template ngTreeItemGroup [ownedBy]="parentItem" #group="ngTreeItemGroup">
            <li ngTreeItem [parent]="group" value="child">Child</li>
          </ng-template>
        </ul>
      </li>
    </ul>
  `,
  imports: [Tree, TreeItem, TreeItemGroup],
  changeDetection: ChangeDetectionStrategy.Eager,
})
class StaticNestedTree {}

describe("Tree destruction", () => {
  it("destroys a flat tree before first change detection", () => {
    const fixture = TestBed.createComponent(StaticFlatTree);
    expect(() => fixture.destroy()).not.toThrow();
  });

  it("destroys a nested tree before first change detection", () => {
    const fixture = TestBed.createComponent(StaticNestedTree);
    expect(() => fixture.destroy()).not.toThrow();
  });

  it("destroys an initialized flat tree (control)", async () => {
    const fixture = TestBed.createComponent(StaticFlatTree);
    await fixture.whenStable();
    expect(() => fixture.destroy()).not.toThrow();
  });

  it("destroys an initialized nested tree (control)", async () => {
    const fixture = TestBed.createComponent(StaticNestedTree);
    await fixture.whenStable();
    expect(() => fixture.destroy()).not.toThrow();
  });
});
```

Do not call `fixture.detectChanges()` or await `fixture.whenStable()` before `destroy()` in the first two tests: that initializes the bindings and bypasses the reproduction. Use static template elements; putting the items inside `@if`/`@for` changes when they are created.

The `ngTreeItemGroup` directive itself is created with the static template, even though its child item inside `ng-template` is deferred.

### Expected Behavior

Destroying before initialization should complete without throwing. Nothing was registered in `ngOnInit`, so there should be nothing to unregister. Destruction after initialization should continue to unregister normally.

### Actual Behavior

The flat-tree test throws:

```text
NG0950: Input "parent" is required but no value is available yet.
```

The nested-tree test throws:

```text
NG0950: Input "ownedBy" is required but no value is available yet.
```

With the four tests in the reproduction, the Chromium suite reports **144 passed, 2 failed**. Only the two pre-initialization tests fail; both initialized control tests pass.

#### Relevant cleanup code

`TreeItem.ngOnDestroy()` reads `parent()` and `tree()`, and `TreeItemGroup.ngOnDestroy()` reads `ownedBy()`. Registration happens in `ngOnInit`.

- [TreeItem at the tested revision](https://github.com/angular/components/blob/e59fce84b/src/aria/tree/tree-item.ts)
- [TreeItemGroup at the tested revision](https://github.com/angular/components/blob/e59fce84b/src/aria/tree/tree-item-group.ts)

#### Related issue and PR

This appears to be the same lifecycle issue as #33972, fixed for combobox in #33974. A corresponding tree fix can retain the references used during registration and only unregister them if initialization registered them. The implementation and regression tests are in #34059.

This report covers `@angular/aria/tree` only.

### Environment

- Components source: angular/components at `e59fce84b` (2026-11-04)
- Angular framework packages used by the repository: 22.3.0-next.3
- Browser: Chromium provided by the repository Bazel browser test setup
- Operating system: macOS, Darwin 25.5.0, arm64
- Node.js: 22.23.4 (`.nvmrc`)
- pnpm: 12.16.1 (`packageManager`)
- Command: `pnpm test src/aria/tree --no-watch`
- Regression status: unknown; older releases have not been tested.

### Is this a regression?

- [ ] Yes, this behavior used to work in the previous version

### The previous version in which this bug was not present was

_No response_

### Description

A combobox that filters its listbox's options can select an option that the filter has just removed. If Enter is pressed in the same render as the change that removes the active option (a fast typist, a slow device, or tools that send keys in a burst), the listbox selects the removed option and emits it from `valueChange`. A render later the listbox's own cleanup drops the value again, so `valueChange` fires `['apple']` and then `[]`.

For a consumer that acts on the first emission, the result is a choice the user never saw. In our app a command palette navigated to a page that was not in the list, and a form combobox saved a value that was not in the list (the user typed "ist" and got "Erbil central").

As far as we can tell from the 22.2.3 sources (the same code is in 22.2.4):

- The listbox learns its options through a `MutationObserver` (`_collection.startObserving(this.element)`). Its `items()` therefore lag a DOM change until the observer fires.
- The reset of an active item that is no longer in `items()` runs in a later `afterRenderEffect` (`listbox.mjs`, the effect that calls `listBehavior.unfocus()` and `setDefaultState()`).
- The combobox's relayed Enter is dispatched from its own `afterRenderEffect` (`keyboardEventRelayEffect`). That happens in the render that removed the option, before either of the above has caught up, so the Enter acts on the stale `activeItem`.

Pressing Enter after one more render selects the right option.

Related: #33973 (the same relay, keys merged within a render). It is a separate mechanism, but the two combine: a quick Down + Enter reaches the listbox as a lone Enter on the stale active item.

### Reproduction

```ts
import { ApplicationRef, Component, computed, signal } from '@angular/core';
import { TestBed } from '@angular/core/testing';
import { Combobox, ComboboxPopup, ComboboxWidget } from '@angular/aria/combobox';
import { Listbox, Option } from '@angular/aria/listbox';

@Component({
  imports: [Combobox, ComboboxPopup, ComboboxWidget, Listbox, Option],
  template: `
    <input ngCombobox #combobox="ngCombobox" [alwaysExpanded]="true" [(value)]="query" />
    <ng-template ngComboboxPopup [combobox]="combobox">
      <div ngListbox ngComboboxWidget #listbox="ngListbox" focusMode="activedescendant"
        selectionMode="explicit" [activeDescendant]="listbox.activeDescendant()"
        (valueChange)="changes.push($event)">
        @for (o of shown(); track o) {
          <div ngOption [value]="o" [label]="o">{{ o }}</div>
        }
      </div>
    </ng-template>
  `,
})
class Repro {
  readonly query = signal('');
  readonly shown = computed(() => ['apple', 'banana', 'cherry'].filter((o) => o.includes(this.query())));
  readonly changes: (readonly string[])[] = [];
}

it('Enter in the render that filters selects the removed option', async () => {
  const fixture = TestBed.createComponent(Repro);
  document.body.appendChild(fixture.nativeElement);
  const stable = () => TestBed.inject(ApplicationRef).whenStable();
  await stable();
  const input = fixture.nativeElement.querySelector('input') as HTMLInputElement;
  input.focus();

  input.value = 'cherry';                                   // filters out apple, the active option
  input.dispatchEvent(new Event('input', { bubbles: true }));
  input.dispatchEvent(new KeyboardEvent('keydown', { key: 'Enter', bubbles: true })); // same task
  await stable();
  await stable();

  expect(fixture.componentInstance.changes).toEqual([['cherry']]);
  // Actual: [['apple'], []]; the DOM shows only 'cherry'.
  // With `await stable()` between the input event and Enter: [['cherry']].
});
```

We saw it in Chromium too (Playwright, the last letter and Enter sent in one burst through CDP): 7 runs in 10 selected the removed option.

### Expected behavior

Enter selects the option that is rendered and active, or does nothing when the active option has been removed. It never selects or emits an option that is no longer in the list.

### Actual behavior

`valueChange` emits the removed option (`['apple']`), then `[]` a render later.

### Environment

- Angular: 22.2.3 (`@angular/aria` 22.2.3; the relevant code is unchanged in 22.2.4)
- Zoneless
- Chromium (Playwright 1.64) and jsdom (Vitest 5)

### Workaround

Ignore a `valueChange` whose value is not among the options currently rendered.

### Is this a regression?

- [ ] Yes, this behavior used to work in the previous version

### The previous version in which this bug was not present was

_No response_

### Description

### Problem

When the default value of a date input is empty, calling `control.reset()` (which can be called directly or by a parent) does not clear invalid text.

This may be a dupe of #33610 which was closed due to inactivity

### Potential Cause

Looks like this may have introduced by a change in the `writeValue` function on `MatDatepickerInputBase`

The below will no longer format the input field if the control value is currently empty and the input has invalid text.

```typescript

  writeValue(value: D): void {
    // We produce a different date object on each keystroke which can cause signal forms'
    // interop logic to keep calling `writeValue` with the same value as the user is typing.
    // Skip such cases since they can prevent the user from typing (see #32442 and #32475).
    this._assignValueProgrammatically(value, value !== this.value);
  }

  protected _assignValueProgrammatically(value: D | null, reformat: boolean) {
    value = this._dateAdapter.deserialize(value);
    this._lastValueValid = this._isValidValue(value);
    value = this._dateAdapter.getValidDateOrNull(value);
    this._assignValue(value);

    // date adapter turns invalid dates to null so `reformat` from `writeValue` is false
    if (reformat) {
      this._formatValue(value);
    }
  }  
```

In previous versions, `_formatValue` would always be called.

### Reproduction

StackBlitz link: https://stackblitz.com/edit/1hicipgd

Steps to reproduce:
1. Enter in any invalid date text into the field tied to a form control
2. Call `control.reset()`  (`Reset Datepicker` button in StackBlitz)

### Expected Behavior

Control should reset to default value (empty in this case) and the invalid input text should be cleared

### Actual Behavior

Invalid value text remains in the input

### Environment

- Angular: 22.2.0
- CDK/Material: 22.2.0
- Browser(s): Chrome 155.0.8059.85 
- Operating System: Windows

### Is this a regression?

- [ ] Yes, this behavior used to work in the previous version

### The previous version in which this bug was not present was

